# CVS08D — Explain v3 ("Learn" depth): what changed in AHA 2025 — adult BLS, adult ALS, post-arrest care, special circumstances, what did not change, superseded numbers,
# paediatric changes, the newborn chain of care, cord management, the ethics chapter, education science and the 2025 prognostication additions.
# No mnemonics or hooks. Driven by qpack/CVS08D.md (10 question links, all DocT; ACLS-60, -61, -68 and -70 two each, ACLS-62 and -65 one each). ACLS-59, -63, -64, -66, -67 and -69
# have no linked question and get short explanations with [pattern] decode items. No Top-up cards for this deck. No `fix` entries for CVS08D in trace/test/data/qbank.json (checked 19 Nov 2026).
# cvs_cardlayer.json: only ACLS-65 carries a line (paediatric compression rate/depth/ratio), which the explanation agrees with; ACLS-61 and -70 carry chips only.
# Checked online on 19 Nov 2026:
#  - AHA 2025 Guidelines Highlights (cpr.heart.org PDF 252500): one chain of survival for adult and paediatric IHCA and OHCA (links shown: recognition and emergency activation, high-quality
#    CPR, defibrillation, advanced resuscitation, post-cardiac arrest care, recovery and survivorship; text starts with prevention and preparedness); choking 5 back blows alternated with 5
#    abdominal thrusts, chest thrusts in late pregnancy or when the abdomen cannot be encircled; visible chest rise; mechanical CPR not routine, may be considered where manual CPR is hard or
#    dangerous; knee level; obesity same technique; head tilt–chin lift in trauma if jaw thrust and adjunct fail; bra may be adjusted; 30:2 rationale = checking chest rise; IV first, IO if IV
#    fails; epinephrine after failed initial defibrillation; vasopressin no advantage; β-blockers/bretylium/procainamide/sotalol uncertain; AF ≥200 J, flutter 200 J, double sequential
#    cardioversion uncertain; vector change and DSD not established after ≥3 shocks; head-up CPR not outside trials; universal TOR in tiered EMS (not EMS-witnessed, no shock, no ROSC);
#    synchronised cardioversion for unstable WCT and for stable WCT when vagal manoeuvres and drugs fail; transvenous pacing for refractory unstable bradycardia. Post-arrest: MAP ≥65 (four RCTs),
#    ≥36 h temperature control (32–34 hypothermic or normothermic/fever prevention), SpO2 90–98% (PaO2 60–105), PCO2 35–45, glucose 70–180 mg/dL, head-to-pelvis CT, echo/POCUS, no treatment of
#    myoclonus without EEG correlate, continuous EEG background without discharges within 72 h supports favourable outcome, NfL added, prognosis ≥72 h from ROSC or normothermia, temporary MCS in
#    selected refractory shock, structured distress assessment of survivors and caregivers. Special circumstances: resuscitative delivery complete by 5 min, ECPR in pregnancy, balanced massive
#    transfusion in AFE, LVAD compressions if perfusion impaired, public-access and take-home naloxone, ECLS and volatile anaesthetics in asthma, HOPE/ICE scores, ice-water immersion ≥0.15 °C/min,
#    IV calcium in hyperkalaemic arrest not well established. Paediatrics: two-finger technique dropped, pauses <10 s, early epinephrine in non-shockable rhythms, DBP ≥25 infants / ≥30 children,
#    post-arrest SBP and MAP >10th centile, child FBAO back blows + abdominal thrusts, infant back blows + chest thrusts, no ETCO2 cut-off alone to stop. Neonatal: seven-link chain of care;
#    deferred clamping ≥60 s term (benefit) and preterm (recommended, lower mortality); intact cord milking ≥35 weeks non-vigorous; SpO2 table starts at 2 min. Ethics: principlism, portable orders,
#    default CPR with three exceptions, family presence, institutional committee or ethics consultant, organ donation. Education: feedback devices, VR, cognitive aids, training under 12 years.
#  - AHA 2025 Part 7 Adult BLS (cpr.heart.org): depth ≥5 cm (1), avoid >6 cm; rate 100–120 (2a); 30:2 (2a) because pauses allow chest rise to be seen; visible chest rise (2a); hypo- and
#    hyperventilation 3: Harm; mechanical CPR routine 3: No Benefit, selected settings 2b; knee level (1), firm surface (2a); obesity same technique (1), more force may be reasonable (2b); head
#    tilt–chin lift in trauma if jaw thrust + adjunct fail (1); choking back blows then abdominal thrusts (1); pads anterolateral or anteroposterior (2a), pad size >8 cm; adjust bra (2b).
#  - AHA 2025 Part 9 Adult ALS (cpr.heart.org): IV first (1, A), IO if IV fails (2a), central 2b; epinephrine after failed initial shocks (2a), non-shockable as soon as feasible (2a); vasopressin
#    3: No Benefit; amiodarone or lidocaine 2b; AF ≥200 J (2a), flutter 200 J (2b); vector change and DSD 2b; head-up CPR 3: No Benefit; atropine for unstable bradycardia (2a); transvenous
#    pacing (2a); bradycardia now defined as <50/min (historically <60).
#  - AHA 2025 adult cardiac arrest algorithm, text version (cpr.heart.org): epinephrine 1 mg every 3–5 min; amiodarone 300 then 150 mg; lidocaine 1–1.5 then 0.5–0.75 mg/kg; biphasic 120–200 J
#    or maximum, monophasic 360 J; depth at least 5 cm, rate 100–120; 30:2, 1 breath every 6 s with an advanced airway; 5 H’s and 5 T’s. AHA 2025 bradycardia algorithm: atropine 1 mg, repeat
#    every 3–5 min, maximum 3 mg; dopamine 5–20 µg/kg/min; epinephrine 2–10 µg/min. AHA ACLS provider manual change notice: avoid atropine after heart transplant.
#  - AHA 2025 Part 11 Post-cardiac arrest care (cpr.heart.org): MAP ≥65 (1), four RCTs of ≤70 vs ≥71 mmHg, SBP target removed; protocolised temperature control (1), 32–37.5 °C (1), ≥36 h (2a),
#    rewarming ≤0.5 °C/h (2b); SpO2 90–98% (2a); PaCO2 35–45 (1); glucose 70–180 (2b, no specific range); 12-lead ECG (1); head-to-pelvis CT (2b, yield about 30%); echo (2b); EEG for seizures and
#    myoclonus (1), treat clinical seizures (1), non-convulsive (2a), no prophylaxis (3); myoclonus without EEG correlate 3: No Benefit; multimodal (1), delayed (1), ≥72 h after normothermia and
#    sedation off (2a); NfL and NSE high within 72 h may support unfavourable outcome (2b), no NfL threshold; normal NSE and withdrawal or better motor response may support favourable (2b);
#    MCS in selected refractory shock 2b, routine 3: No Benefit.
#  - AHA 2025 Part 6 Paediatric BLS (cpr.heart.org): heel of one hand or two thumb-encircling hands (1), two-finger eliminated for ineffective depth; depth one-third AP, about 4 cm infants, 5 cm
#    children (2a); rate 100–120 (2a); 30:2 single, 15:2 two rescuers (2a); infant FBAO back blows + chest thrusts with the heel of the hand (1), no abdominal thrusts; child FBAO now starts with
#    back blows (1); no blind finger sweeps.
#  - AHA 2025 Part 13 Resuscitation education science (cpr.heart.org): feedback devices in training, HCP and lay (1, A); VR for knowledge (2b), VR for skills 3: Harm; cognitive aids for HCP (2a),
#    lay 3: No Benefit (30–70 s delays to calling and compressions); start training under 12 years (1).
#  - Irish Heart Foundation 2025 ACLS science summary table (2020 vs 2025): SpO2 92–98 → 90–98%; TTM 32–36 °C for 24 h → 32–37.5 °C for ≥36 h.
#  - AHA 2025 Part 10 special circumstances (hypothermia: defibrillate once, defer further shocks and epinephrine until ≥30 °C, 2b) as checked for CVS08C the same day.
# From AHA 2020 and standard references without a fresh check (confident unless said): 2020 IV first 2a; 2020 post-arrest avoid SBP <90 and MAP <65; 2020 vasopressin 2b ‘may be considered,
# no advantage’; 2020 DSD 2b; 2020 mechanical CPR ‘not routine’; 2020 depth ≥5 cm and avoid >6 cm; 2020 paediatric post-arrest SBP >5th centile; 2015/2010 algorithm cardioversion energies
# (narrow regular 50–100 J, narrow irregular 120–200 J); BLS/ALS TOR rule criteria; TTM2 (2021), BOX (2022), DOSE-VF (2022), PARAMEDIC-3 and IVIO (2024), PREMOD (2019); neonatal SpO2 table;
# NfL as an axonal protein; ERC–ESICM 2025 SpO2 94–98%.
# Second, independent review 19 Nov 2026 (cpr.heart.org 2025 Parts: adult BLS, adult ALS, post-cardiac arrest care, special circumstances, paediatric BLS and ALS, neonatal,
#  systems of care, education; AHA 2020 Part 3 and Part 4 text on ahajournals.org; AHA 2023 focused update on adult ACLS): shockable-rhythm epinephrine 2b (2020) → 2a (2025);
#  IO 2b → 2a; 32–37.5 °C range dates from 2023; public-access naloxone wording (systems of care, class 1 policy, 2a distribution); paediatric DBP 25/30 in 2020 supporting text, explicit
#  target (2b) in 2025; ERC–ESICM 2025 fever prevention ≤37.5 °C for 36–72 h. 2020 recommendation tables could not be opened online: the 2020 classes for IV first (2a), vasopressin (2b),
#  post-arrest SBP <90/MAP <65 (2b) and paediatric SBP >5th centile (1) are from the published 2020 text as remembered, consistent with what the 2020 supporting text and 2025 text say.

SPINE = [
  ("BLS: simpler, ventilation-aware CPR", "Adult BLS 2025 keeps the core numbers but unifies the chain, puts back blows first in choking, defines a breath by visible chest rise and drops gadgets that do not help."),
  ("ALS: IV first, fewer gadgets", "ALS 2025 makes IV the first route, times epinephrine by rhythm, raises cardioversion energy and declines to endorse vector change, double shocks or head-up CPR."),
  ("Post-arrest: longer, warmer, two-sided", "After ROSC the targets moved: SpO2 90–98%, MAP 65 or more, temperature control 32–37.5 °C for at least 36 hours, and prognostication now looks for good signs as well as bad."),
  ("Special circumstances get algorithms", "Pregnancy, LVADs, opioids, asthma, cold, heat and hyperkalaemia each change the algorithm because the cause, the circulation or the response to drugs is different."),
  ("Children and newborns", "Paediatric CPR loses the two-finger technique and gains early epinephrine and centile-based blood pressure targets, while the newborn gets its own chain of care built around ventilation and cord management."),
  ("Constants, old numbers, ethics, teaching", "Most drug doses and the 30:2 ratio stayed, five numbers moved, and two new chapters set the ethical defaults and how CPR should be taught."),
]

PRIMER = "AHA guidelines grade each statement by Class of Recommendation (1 = should / is recommended, 2a = is reasonable, 2b = may be considered, 3: No Benefit or 3: Harm) and by level of evidence; a ‘change’ can be a new number, a new topic, or the same advice moved up or down a class. The 2025 Guidelines for CPR and ECC (Circulation, October 2025) are the first full rewrite since 2020, and the European Resuscitation Council published its own 2025 guidelines with some different figures (for example post-ROSC SpO2 94–98%). Question banks written before late 2025 still carry 2020 numbers, so in a transition year you must know both the old and the new figure and which guideline a question is keyed to. CVS08A to CVS08C cover the algorithms themselves; this deck is the change log."

