    ('b', "**VR: may be reasonable for KNOWLEDGE acquisition; VR should NOT be used to teach CPR SKILLS.** Knowledge 2b; skills 3: Harm, because without a real manikin there is no resistance to push against, and trainees learn the wrong depth and force."),
    ('c', "**Cognitive aids: may be reasonable for HEALTH CARE PROFESSIONALS; NOT recommended for LAY RESCUERS — they delayed the start of CPR.** Professionals (2a) gain adherence; lay users (3: No Benefit) delayed calling and starting compressions by 30–70 seconds in trials."),
    ('d', "**Start CPR training in children YOUNGER THAN 12 YEARS. Also: rapid-cycle deliberate practice, teamwork competencies, gamified learning, scripted debriefing.** Younger children may not yet reach adult depth, but the guideline’s reason for early training (class 1) is to increase willingness and self-confidence to act in later years."),
